diff -ruN data/skills_1000/tessel-frontend/SKILL.md data2/skills_1000/tessel-frontend/SKILL.md
--- data/skills_1000/tessel-frontend/SKILL.md	2026-10-17 15:07:27.468583121 +0000
+++ data2/skills_1000/tessel-frontend/SKILL.md	2026-10-18 00:46:55.796913885 +0000
@@ -509,6 +509,9 @@
 Each part has `src/parts/<kebab-name>/<kebab-name>.spec.tsx` that imports `renderPart` from `@tessel/testkit` and has at least one `it(` block. That's the floor, not the ceiling — `tessel doctor` warns (never fails) when a part
 directory has no spec file.
 
+Drive interactions in a spec through the `user` that `renderPart` returns: `const { user, getByRole } = renderPart(<Part />); await user.click(getByRole("button"));` and make the `it` async. Never call `element.click()` or `fireEvent` directly in a part spec: those updates land outside React's act, are not flushed, and the assertion after them reads stale DOM. This is the single most common reason a part spec fails in CI.
+
+
 ### Snapshots
 
 Snapshots are allowed; unversioned drift is not.
